<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="srl_dual">

    <!-- Import srl_dual urdf file.

         THIS ALREADY BRINGS ITS OWN ros2_control. `load_arm` in
         kortex_description emits one system per arm
         (left_KortexMultiInterfaceHardware, right_KortexMultiInterfaceHardware)
         declaring all seven joints of that arm, and `load_gripper` emits one
         per gripper. Between them every joint in the robot is covered, with
         the arms' initial positions taken from the URDF's `initial_positions`
         blocks, which are the home pose. -->
    <xacro:include filename="$(find srl_description)/urdf/srl_dual.urdf.xacro" />

    <!-- THE SETUP ASSISTANT `FakeSystem` BLOCK IS DELETED, 2026-08-16, AND
         THIS COMMENT IS WHY.

         It was Setup Assistant boilerplate: a second ros2_control system
         re-declaring all fourteen arm joints, with their initial positions
         read from `initial_positions.yaml`, a file holding FOURTEEN ZEROS. It
         was a SIXTH copy of the home pose, holding the wrong value, and
         `test_home_has_one_source` did not know it existed because it lives
         in a different package.

         It never took effect, and the reason it never took effect is the
         problem. Measured on every boot:

             controller_manager: Loading hardware 'FakeSystem'
             [WARN] Tried to insert StateInterface with already existing key.
                    Insert[left_joint_1/position]        ... 29 of these
             [ERROR] Tried to insert CommandInterface with already existing
                     key. Insert[right_joint_7/position]

         The per-arm blocks load FIRST, claim the interfaces, and FakeSystem's
         duplicates are rejected one by one. So the home pose survived BY LOAD
         ORDER, on a warning, with an error alongside it. Had the ordering
         gone the other way, all fourteen joints would have come up at 0.000,
         which is failure mode 1 in docs/system/home_wrist_is_real.md ("a
         stray publisher makes every joint read 0.000, which is also
         plausible") and which three sessions spent their time looking for.

         Removing it costs nothing: every joint it declared is declared by a
         block that is actually used, and the boot is now clean. The
         `initial_positions_file` argument goes with it; nothing else in
         srl_* referenced either. -->

</robot>
